WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.296
A secure upload design starts before
choosing a Servlet API,

2
00:00:03.376 --> 00:00:05.787
Spring MultipartFile, storage client, or
scanner.

3
00:00:05.867 --> 00:00:08.663
First identify who can upload files, what
files are allowed,

4
00:00:08.743 --> 00:00:11.264
why the files are needed, and who can
later access them.

5
00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:14.469
A profile picture, resume, invoice,
support attachment, tax form,

6
00:00:14.549 --> 00:00:18.112
diagnostic archive, and administrative
import file do not have the same risk

7
00:00:18.192 --> 00:00:18.630
profile.

8
00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:21.506
Each use case deserves its own allowed
types, maximum sizes,

9
00:00:21.586 --> 00:00:24.765
retention rules, scanning expectations,
and authorization model.

10
00:00:24.845 --> 00:00:27.640
Map the path from client to reverse proxy,
Java application,

11
00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.283
multipart parser, temporary storage,
validation step, scanning workflow,

12
00:00:31.363 --> 00:00:34.159
permanent storage, metadata database,
background worker,

13
00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:35.774
and download or preview feature.

14
00:00:35.854 --> 00:00:37.772
That map shows where trust changes hands.

15
00:00:37.852 --> 00:00:41.360
Browsers, API clients, load balancers,
servlet containers, controllers,

16
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.345
temporary directories, object stores,
scanners, databases,

17
00:00:44.425 --> 00:00:47.276
and users all sit on different sides of
different boundaries.

18
00:00:47.356 --> 00:00:50.864
The design should say what each boundary
is allowed to accept and what it must

19
00:00:50.944 --> 00:00:51.327
verify.

20
00:00:51.407 --> 00:00:53.764
Separate public uploads, authenticated
uploads,

21
00:00:53.844 --> 00:00:58.010
administrative uploads, service-to-service
uploads, and internal processing queues.

22
00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:01.982
An anonymous contact-form attachment is
not the same as a privileged administrator

23
00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:03.049
importing a CSV file.

24
00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:06.637
A tenant-specific customer document is not
the same as a public image asset.

25
00:01:06.717 --> 00:01:07.977
Global rules can be useful,

26
00:01:08.057 --> 00:01:12.004
but upload security is usually strongest
when requirements are defined per workflow

27
00:01:12.084 --> 00:01:14.441
rather than applied vaguely to every file
feature.

28
00:01:14.521 --> 00:01:18.413
Decide whether files are processed
synchronously, asynchronously, quarantined,

29
00:01:18.493 --> 00:01:19.096
or rejected.

30
00:01:19.176 --> 00:01:21.642
A low-risk image may be checked and
released quickly.

31
00:01:21.722 --> 00:01:25.450
A higher-risk document may need to be held
until scanning and content validation

32
00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:25.913
finish.

33
00:01:25.993 --> 00:01:29.721
A failed scan or failed conversion should
not make the file available by default.

34
00:01:29.801 --> 00:01:32.487
Documenting the lifecycle gives
developers, reviewers,

35
00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:36.020
and operators a shared model for
implementing and supporting the feature.
